# git — submodule flows (init / update / bump / remove)

0.1.0 Use when a repo contains git submodules and you need to clone it correctly, update a submodule pointer, add a new one, or remove one. Submodules have unusual lifecycle commands; this is the cheat sheet.

DESTRUCTIVE: maybe

Working in a repo with `.gitmodules` (or about to add a submodule). The flow is unusual enough that even experienced users reach for docs every time.

If your repo uses `git subtree` instead, that's a different tool — submodules track external SHAs; subtrees merge external history into your repo's history.

app :git__submodule_flows_init_update_bump_remove do
  title       "git — submodule flows (init / update / bump / remove)"
  description "0.1.0 Use when a repo contains git submodules and you need to clone it correctly, update a submodule pointer, add a new one, or remove one. — NETWORK"
end

## Clone a repo that has submodules

### confirm submodules exist
```bash
  test -f .gitmodules || { echo "no .gitmodules — repo has no submodules"; exit 1; }
  cat .gitmodules
```

### the one-shot incantation
```bash
  git clone --recurse-submodules <url>
```

### if you already cloned without --recurse
```bash
  git submodule update --init --recursive
```

## Update a submodule to a newer upstream commit

### pull the latest from the submodule's tracked branch
```bash
  git submodule update --remote --merge -- path/to/submodule
  # then commit the bump in the OUTER repo so the SHA-pointer moves
  git add path/to/submodule
  git commit -m "bump submodule: <reason>"
```

Without `--remote`, `git submodule update` just resets the submodule to the SHA your outer repo already points at — which is the right call after pulling someone else's commit that bumped the pointer.

## Add a new submodule

### register + clone the submodule in one step
```bash
  git submodule add https://github.com/owner/repo.git path/to/new-sub
  git commit -m "add submodule: <name>"
  # creates .gitmodules entry + checks out the submodule + commits
```

## Remove a submodule (the painful flow)

Removing requires touching 3 places: `.gitmodules`, `.git/config`, and the working tree.

### 1. de-init the submodule (clears .git/config entry)
```bash
  git submodule deinit -f path/to/sub
```

### 2. remove from index (clears .gitmodules entry)
```bash
  git rm -f path/to/sub
```

### 3. clean up .git/modules cache (else re-add fails)
```bash
  rm -rf .git/modules/path/to/sub
```

### 4. commit
```bash
  git commit -m "remove submodule: <name>"
```

## Working with submodules inside worktrees

Each worktree needs its own submodule init. After =git worktree add ...= you typically need:

```bash
  cd ../new-worktree
  git submodule update --init --recursive
```

See [[worktree-management]] for the worktree side.

## Common pitfalls

1. *Forgot `--recurse-submodules` on clone.* Repo looks
     broken (missing files in submodule paths). Fix: =git submodule update --init --recursive=.

2. *Bumped submodule but forgot to commit the bump.* The
     submodule directory shows "new commits" in `git status` but you never `git add` the path. Pull in your team sees the OLD SHA. Fix: `git add <path> && git commit`.

3. *Re-adding a submodule that wasn't fully removed.* If
     step 3 (`rm -rf .git/modules/<path>`) was skipped, git complains "already exists in the index". Run step 3 then retry add.

4. *Detached HEAD inside submodule.* Default state. Don't
     panic — submodule checkouts are SHA-pinned, not branch- tracking. If you want to ITERATE on the submodule, =cd <sub> && git checkout main= (or whatever), then bump.

5. *Modified submodule contents not staged.* Changes inside
     a submodule's working tree don't get added by =git add .= in the outer repo. `cd` into the submodule + commit there, push, then update the pointer in the outer repo.

## Verification checklist

- [ ] `git status` clean after the operation
- [ ] `.gitmodules` reflects intent (paths + URLs match what you wanted)
- [ ] Submodule dirs are populated (`ls path/to/sub/ should not be empty`)
- [ ] If updated: outer repo committed the new SHA pointer

